Data Prep (0.5 pts)
Download the “hospital_sim.csv” dataset from Canvas, add it to your project folder, and read it into R as a tibble called dat_raw. Use the code below to prepare the data for multilevel analysis. In your Quarto file, annotate this code by adding comments explaining what each line does.

dat_l2 &lt;- 
  dat_raw |&gt;
  summarize(
    # COMMENT
    .by = hospital,
    # COMMENT
    nurse_ratio = first(nurse_ratio),
    # COMMENT
    hospital_type = first(hospital_type),
    # COMMENT
    wait_time_b = mean(wait_time, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt; 
  mutate(
    # COMMENT
    nurse_ratio_c = center(nurse_ratio),
    # COMMENT
    wait_time_bc = center(wait_time_b)
  ) |&gt; 
  print()

dat &lt;- 
  dat_raw |&gt; 
  # COMMENT
  select(-c(nurse_ratio, hospital_type)) |&gt; 
  # COMMENT
  left_join(dat_l2, by = "hospital") |&gt;
  # COMMENT
  mutate(wait_time_wc = wait_time - wait_time_b) |&gt; 
  print()

1. Data Prep (1.0 pts)
Download the “mood_sim.csv” dataset from Canvas, add it to your project folder, and read it into R as a tibble called dat_raw. Use the code below to prepare the data for analysis. In your Quarto file, annotate this code by adding comments explaining what each step does.

dat_l2 &lt;- 
  dat_raw |&gt;
  # COMMENT
  summarize(
    .by = subject,
    sleep_b = mean(sleep, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |&gt; 
  # COMMENT
  mutate(sleep_bc = center(sleep_b))

dat &lt;- 
  dat_raw |&gt;
  # COMMENT
  left_join(dat_l2, by = "subject") |&gt; 
  mutate(
    # COMMENT
    sleep_w = sleep - sleep_b,
    # COMMENT
    workout = factor(workout)
  ) |&gt; 
  # COMMENT
  arrange(subject, studyday_frac) |&gt; 
  mutate(
    # COMMENT
    time_d = factor(studyday),
    # COMMENT
    time_c = numFactor(studyday_frac)
  )
